  ($q$cs-79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on desert ecosystems opens by describing a single cactus surviving a two-year drought, then broadens to explain how root adaptations across desert plant species allow such survival, and closes by discussing conservation efforts to protect desert habitats.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It begins with a specific case, moves to broader supporting evidence, and ends with practical recommendations.","It presents two competing hypotheses and dismisses one entirely.","It opens with a policy proposal and closes with an unrelated anecdote.","It compares two desert ecosystems without discussing conservation."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text moves from one cactus's survival (specific case) to root adaptations across species (broader evidence) to conservation efforts (proposed action), matching the correct answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A profile of a chess champion opens with a description of her losing her first tournament match at age seven, then explains that the loss motivated years of disciplined practice that eventually led to her international title.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It connects an early setback described in the first sentence to the champion's later success.","It contradicts the claim made in the first sentence.","It introduces a completely unrelated childhood memory.","It states the profile's conclusion before any details are given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence explains how the early loss described in the first sentence motivated the practice that led to her later success, linking the two sentences.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on rooftop gardens begins by noting that many cities lose green space to development, then describes how several buildings have converted unused roof areas into small farms that supply nearby restaurants.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a concrete response to the problem introduced in the first sentence.","It disputes the claim that cities are losing green space.","It shifts to a discussion of a completely different urban issue.","It concludes the article without offering any supporting detail."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence directly answers the problem raised in the first sentence by describing rooftop farms as a concrete response.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on the Japanese tea ceremony opens with a step-by-step description of preparing a single bowl of matcha, then explains the philosophical concept of ichigo ichie, or the idea that each gathering is unrepeatable, and closes by reflecting on how this idea might apply to everyday encounters.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a concrete ritual to its underlying philosophy to a broader life lesson.","It presents two rival ceremonial traditions and argues for one over the other.","It opens with a philosophical claim and ends with a technical manual.","It compares tea ceremonies across several unrelated countries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay's stated progression — ritual, then philosophy, then a life lesson — matches the correct answer exactly.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$In an essay defending a controversial urban rezoning plan, the author spends the opening paragraph summarizing residents' objections in detail before spending the remaining paragraphs addressing each objection point by point.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Acknowledging opposing objections in detail before responding to strengthen the author's case","Ignoring residents' objections to avoid weakening the plan's support","Presenting only the author's opinion without referencing any objections","Avoiding a clear position so readers can form their own conclusions"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Summarizing objections in detail before responding to each one is a concession-and-rebuttal strategy, matching the correct answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A profile of an inventor opens with an account of him tinkering with a broken radio as a boy, unable to afford a replacement. Decades later, the profile closes with his company donating refurbished electronics to schools that cannot afford new equipment.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the closing paragraph functions in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It echoes the opening anecdote, linking his early resourcefulness to his later philanthropy.","It introduces a new criticism of his business practices.","It shifts focus away from electronics entirely.","It contradicts the account given in the opening paragraph."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The closing paragraph revisits the resourcefulness from the opening anecdote in the form of philanthropy, creating a deliberate echo, as the correct answer describes.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An article on soil health begins by describing a farmer's dramatically improved crop yields after switching to no-till methods, then presents research data on how tilling disrupts fungal networks that plants rely on for nutrients.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It supplies a scientific explanation for the improvement described in the first sentence.","It contradicts the farmer's reported results.","It introduces an unrelated agricultural practice.","It restates the first sentence without adding new information."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence explains the mechanism (fungal networks) behind the improved yields described in the first sentence, functioning as a scientific explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A retrospective essay first details a painter's early, celebrated landscapes, then spends its remaining pages examining a little-known series of abstract works she produced privately and never exhibited during her lifetime.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To draw attention to an overlooked body of work alongside the painter's celebrated pieces","To argue that the painter's early landscapes were her only significant achievement","To provide technical instructions for landscape painting","To dismiss the value of the painter's private, unexhibited works"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay covers celebrated early work, then draws attention to an overlooked private series, matching the correct answer's description of the purpose.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on a controversial court ruling opens by summarizing the judge's stated reasoning, then spends several paragraphs presenting the historical precedents the judge appears to have overlooked, without ever directly stating that the ruling was incorrect.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Implying a flaw in the ruling by presenting overlooked precedents rather than stating the criticism outright","Praising the ruling directly and providing precedents that support it","Ignoring all legal precedent in favor of personal opinion","Stating a firm conclusion before presenting any precedents"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Presenting overlooked precedents without directly stating the ruling was wrong is an implicit rather than explicit criticism, matching the correct answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay begins by asserting that a river's fish populations had been in permanent decline for a century, then describes recent survey data showing salmon returning in numbers not seen since before industrialization, using the data to challenge the essay's own opening claim.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the survey data in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a counterexample that undermines the assumption stated at the beginning of the essay.","It confirms the assumption stated at the beginning of the essay.","It qualifies the opening claim as true only for the first half of the century rather than overturning it.","It summarizes the essay's conclusion before any evidence is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage states the survey data is used to challenge the essay's own opening claim outright, not merely to narrow its timeframe, so it functions as a counterexample undermining that assumption, matching the correct answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The essay opens by describing the harsh reviews a composer's symphony received at its premiere, then spends the remainder of the piece tracing how the same symphony came to be regarded, decades later, as one of the defining works of its era.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To trace how a work's critical reputation shifted dramatically over time","To argue that the symphony was rightly dismissed by its earliest audiences","To argue that the symphony's harsh initial reviews permanently damaged its legacy","To defend the harsh reviews the symphony initially received"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay traces the symphony's reputation from harsh initial reviews to later acclaim as a defining work, not a permanently damaged legacy, matching the correct answer's description of a shift in reputation.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Botanist A argues that a rare fern species is disappearing primarily because of habitat destruction from logging, citing measured declines that track logging permits issued in the region.

